Q: Getting Add [x] to fillable property error when trying to save a new inventory item? A: <p>Since I don't know what are you using but since your tags is migration I will assume you are using Laravel.</p>
<p>You're getting that error because Laravel protects against mass assignment by default. You just need to explicitly define which columns are allowed to be filled in your Model using the $fillable property. Here is how you should structure your migration to default the quantity to 0, and how to update your Model.</p>
<p>Here's a reference:</p>
<pre><code>First you will create a generic Item with a name, description, and quantity.
1. Create the Model, Migration, and Controller:
Bash
php artisan make:model Item -mcr

2. This is how to set it to zero. Define the Database Table:
Open the newly created migration file in database/migrations/YYYY_MM_DD_create_items_table.php and update the up() method:
PHP
public function up(): void
{
    Schema::create('items', function (Blueprint $table) {
        $table-&gt;id();
        $table-&gt;string('name');
        $table-&gt;text('description')-&gt;nullable();
        $table-&gt;integer('quantity')-&gt;default(0);
        $table-&gt;timestamps();
    });
}

Run the migration:
Bash
php artisan migrate

3. Update the Model:
Open app/Models/Item.php and allow mass assignment:
PHP
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Item extends Model
{
    protected $fillable = ['name', 'description', 'quantity'];
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to overcome Athena/glue varchar limit when using COPY command from parquet A: <p>At least in redshift:</p>
<pre><code>SET json_parse_truncate_strings=ON;
</code></pre>

Q: Katex formulas in gt tables not translating to correct docx formulas A: <p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/76290958/28479453">This</a> suggests that <code>{gt}</code> has issues translating <a href="https://math.meta.stackexchange.com/questions/5020/mathjax-basic-tutorial-and-quick-reference">MathJax</a> formulas or Markdown to Word's <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_Open_XML" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Office Open XML</a>.</p>
<h3>1. {flextable}</h3>
<p>I would suggest using <code>flextable</code> which let's you use <a href="https://davidgohel.github.io/flextable/reference/as_equation.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>as_equation</code></a> to create <em>valid</em> formulas that show correctly in both HTML- and Docx output.</p>
<pre><code>---
title: &quot;Testing&quot;
output:
  word_document: default
  html_document: default
---

```{r, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE}
library(gt)        # for exibble
library(flextable) # for flextable
library(ftExtra)   # for colformat_md formatting markdown
library(equatags)  # To use &quot;as_equation&quot;, package 'equatags' is required; also equatags::mathjax_install()  must be executed only once to install necessary dependencies.

set_flextable_defaults(na_str = &quot;NA&quot;)
flextable(exibble[, c(&quot;currency&quot;, &quot;char&quot;)]) |&gt;
  set_header_labels(currency = &quot;$$Currency_{1}$$&quot;) |&gt;
  # format markdown within header and body!
  colformat_md(part = &quot;all&quot;) |&gt;
  autofit() |&gt;
  set_caption(
    caption = as_paragraph(
      as_equation(&quot;\\dfrac{1}{2}&quot;)
    )
  )
```
</code></pre>
<p>giving</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/Jfq4ySo2.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/Jfq4ySo2.png" alt="table as word output on the left and html output on the right" /></a></p>
<hr />
<h3>2. Using images (hacky)</h3>
<p>A hacky solution could be capturing your <code>gt</code>-table as image using <code>gtsave()</code> which wraps <code>webshot2::webshot</code>. For this to work however, we need to transform your formulas to HTML using <code>gt::html(katex::katex_mathml(&quot;\\frac{1}{2}&quot;))</code> so that the transformed MathJax can be properly captured.</p>
<pre><code>---
title: &quot;Testing&quot;
format: 
    docx: default
    html: default
---

```{r echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, out.width = &quot;200px&quot;}
library(gt)
library(katex)

X &lt;- exibble[, c(&quot;currency&quot;, &quot;char&quot;)] |&gt;
    gt() |&gt;
    tab_header(title = html(katex_mathml(&quot;\\frac{1}{2}&quot;))) |&gt;
    cols_label(
        currency = html(katex_mathml(&quot;Currency_{1}&quot;))
    )
gtsave(X, &quot;table.png&quot;, zoom = 7)
knitr::include_graphics(&quot;table.png&quot;)
```
</code></pre>
<p>giving</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/Z4uOKV6m.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/Z4uOKV6m.png" alt="res2" /></a></p>
<hr />
<h3>3. Replacing $$content$$ with valid MathML inside a <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-sg/answers/questions/5644120/microsoft-word-fails-to-correctly-render-nested-ma" rel="nofollow noreferrer">word run</a></h3>
<p><a href="https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/equatags/versions/0.2.2/topics/transform_mathjax" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>equatags::transform_mathjax()</code></a> provides a way to transform MathJax into 'MathML' XML like</p>
<pre><code>transform_mathjax(x = &quot;Currency_{1}&quot;, to = &quot;mml&quot;) |&gt; cat()

&lt;m:oMath xmlns:m=&quot;http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/math&quot; xmlns:mml=&quot;http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML&quot;&gt;
  &lt;m:r&gt;
    &lt;m:t&gt;Currenc&lt;/m:t&gt;
  &lt;/m:r&gt;
  &lt;m:sSub&gt;
    &lt;m:e&gt;
      &lt;m:r&gt;
        &lt;m:t&gt;y&lt;/m:t&gt;
      &lt;/m:r&gt;
    &lt;/m:e&gt;
    &lt;m:sub&gt;
      &lt;m:r&gt;
        &lt;m:t&gt;1&lt;/m:t&gt;
      &lt;/m:r&gt;
    &lt;/m:sub&gt;
  &lt;/m:sSub&gt;
&lt;/m:oMath&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Now if you would export the gt-table to word-xml you can see clearly that the MathJax <code>Currency_{1}</code> does not get converted to valid OOML what word can read as formula:</p>
<pre><code>exibble[, c(&quot;currency&quot;, &quot;char&quot;)] |&gt;
    gt() |&gt;
    tab_header(title = md(&quot;$$\\frac{1}{2}$$&quot;)) |&gt;
    cols_label(currency = md(&quot;$$Currency_{1}$$&quot;)) |&gt;
    as_word() |&gt; cat()

## ...
&lt;w:t xml:space=&quot;preserve&quot;&gt;$$Currency_{1}$$&lt;/w:t&gt;
## ...
</code></pre>
<p>So the idea is to replace anything inside the <code>$$</code>-tags with above MathML provided by <code>transform_mathjax</code> and then insert these snippets into word run tags. <strong>Note:</strong> This is only done, if you output to Word. If you output to HTML, your <em>normal</em> <code>gt</code>-widget is printed.</p>
<h4>Codes</h4>
<pre><code>---
title: &quot;Testing&quot;
format: 
    docx: default
    html: default
---

```{r echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results='asis'}
library(gt)
library(equatags)
library(gsubfn)

if (!knitr::is_html_output() &amp; !knitr::is_latex_output()){
  replace_math &lt;- \(x) gsubfn(&quot;\\$\\$(.*?)\\$\\$&quot;, \(m) sprintf(&quot;&lt;w:r&gt;%s&lt;/w:r&gt;&quot;, transform_mathjax(m, to=&quot;mml&quot;)), x)

  tab &lt;- exibble[, c(&quot;currency&quot;, &quot;char&quot;)] |&gt;
    gt() |&gt;
    tab_header(title = md(&quot;$$\\frac{1}{2}$$&quot;)) |&gt;
    cols_label(currency = md(&quot;$$Currency_{1}$$&quot;)) |&gt;
    as_word() |&gt; cat()
   cat(&quot;```{=openxml}&quot;, replace_math(tab), &quot;```&quot;, sep = &quot;\n&quot;)
}else{
  exibble[, c(&quot;currency&quot;, &quot;char&quot;)] |&gt;
    gt() |&gt;
    tab_header(title = md(&quot;$$\\frac{1}{2}$$&quot;)) |&gt;
    cols_label(currency = md(&quot;$$Currency_{1}$$&quot;))
}
```
</code></pre>
<p>giving
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/jYZIXNFd.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/jYZIXNFd.png" alt="res" /></a></p>
<p>Caveats:</p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;1/2&quot; is written as word-table capture-text and not centered over the table</li>
<li>There is a security related <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/60098480/28479453">popup-meessage</a> if you open this word-file</li>
</ul>
